Computer Architecture

    CPU architecture is the design and structure of a CPU, including how it is organized and how it processes instructions.

        It includes things like:

            - What instructions the CPU can understand
            - How much data it can process at once, such as 32-bit or 64-bit
            - What registers it has
            - How memory is accessed
            - How programs communicate with the CPU

        Examples of CPU architectures:

            - x86 / x86-64 — common in laptops and desktops
            - ARM — common in phones, tablets, and many modern laptops
            - RISC-V — open-source CPU architecture

    CPU Microarchitecture is how the CPU is actually built internally to follow that architecture.

        It includes parts/components like:

            - Control unit — directs the CPU
            - ALU — does arithmetic and logic
            - Registers — tiny, very fast storage
            - Cache — fast memory inside or near the CPU
            - Cores — processing units inside the CPU
            - Pipelines — help the CPU work on many instruction steps at once
            - Branch predictor — guesses what code will run next

        Two CPUs can use the same architecture but have different microarchitectures. For example, Intel and AMD CPUs both
        use x86-64 architecture, but their internal designs are different.


What is ISA called in theoretically?

    In theoretical terms, an ISA is usually called an: "abstract machine rules" or "hardware–software interface" or
    "programmer-visible architecture". These all describe the same idea from different angles.

        - ISA as an abstract machine rule
            
            The ISA describes a virtual/abstract CPU rules that software can target.

            It defines things like:

                - registers
                - memory model
                - instructions
                - data types
                - addressing modes
                - program counter
                - flags/status registers
                - instruction behavior
                - binary encoding

            For example, the ISA says:

                There is a register called x1.
                There is an instruction called add.
                add x1, x2, x3 means: x1 = x2 + x3.

            That is an abstract rule.

            The real CPU hardware may execute it using pipelines, caches, micro-operations, branch predictors, etc.,
            but the ISA only describes the visible behavior.

        - ISA vs microarchitecture

            Important difference:

                | Concept               | Theoretical meaning                     |
                | --------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
                | **ISA**               | Abstract machine rules                  |
                | **Microarchitecture** | Physical/internal implementation        |
                | **Assembly**          | Human-readable form of ISA instructions |
                | **Machine code**      | Binary encoding of ISA instructions     |

            Example:

                ISA: "There is an ADD instruction."
                Microarchitecture: "Here is how this CPU physically performs ADD."

            Two CPUs can have the same ISA but different microarchitecture.

                For example:

                    Intel x86-64 CPU
                    AMD x86-64 CPU

                Both understand the same general ISA, but internally they are built differently.

    ISA analogy:

        | Computer concept      | Language analogy                                     |
        | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
        | **ISA**               | The language rules: vocabulary, grammar, meaning     |
        | **Assembly language** | Human-readable sentences written using that language |
        | **Machine code**      | The same sentences encoded in binary                 |
        | **Assembler**         | A translator from assembly into machine code         |


ISA families

    An ISA family is a group of related CPU instruction rulebooks.

        For example:

            | ISA family        | Examples inside the family   | Common CPUs                                   |
            | ----------------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
            | **x86 family**    | x86, IA-32, x86-64           | Intel Core, AMD Ryzen                         |
            | **ARM family**    | ARMv7, ARMv8, ARMv9, AArch64 | Apple M chips, Snapdragon, Raspberry Pi       |
            | **RISC-V family** | RV32, RV64, plus extensions  | microcontrollers, research CPUs, custom chips |

        So an ISA family is not always one exact ISA. It is more like a family tree of related instruction systems.

    Simple analogy

        Think of ISA families like human language families:

            | Human language idea    | CPU idea                                      |
            | ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
            | Germanic languages     | x86 family                                    |
            | Romance languages      | ARM family                                    |
            | Austronesian languages | RISC-V family                                 |
            | English, German, Dutch | Different versions/variants inside one family |
            | Grammar and vocabulary | Instruction rules and binary encoding         |

    What makes ISA families different?

        They differ in the instruction design style, instruction, register, instruction size, instruction decoding, Memory access style.

            1. Instruction design style: CISC vs RISC

                Traditionally:

                    x86-64 = CISC-style
                    ARM    = RISC-style

                x86-64: CISC-style

                    CISC means Complex Instruction Set Computer. x86-64 has many instructions, and
                    some instructions can do more complex things.

                    Example idea:

                        asm(assembly)
                        
                            add [memory_address], rax

                        This can add a register value directly to a value in memory.
                        So one instruction can combine:

                            read memory
                            add value
                            write result

                ARM: RISC-style

                    RISC means Reduced Instruction Set Computer. ARM usually uses simpler instructions.
                    Arithmetic normally happens between registers, not directly on memory.

                    Example idea:

                        asm(assembly)

                            ldr x1, [memory_address]   ; load from memory
                            add x1, x1, x2             ; add registers
                            str x1, [memory_address]   ; store back to memory

                        So ARM often separates:

                            load
                            calculate
                            store

                Conclution:

                    - x86-64 (CISC-style): one instruction can combine many instructions & one instruction can do more complex things.
                    - ARM (RISC-style): often separates instructions to make simpler instructions.

            2. Different instruction
            Each ISA family has its own instruction names and behavior.

                For example, addition can look different:

                    asm(assembly)
                        
                        ; x86-64
                        add rax, rbx

                            Meaning:

                                rax = rax + rbx
                        
                        ; ARM / AArch64
                        add x0, x1, x2

                            Meaning:

                                x0 = x1 + x2
                        
                        ; RISC-V
                        add x1, x2, x3

                    They all mean “add,” but the exact rules are different.

            3. Different registers
            Registers are tiny fast storage locations inside the CPU.

                Different ISA families expose different registers.

                For example:

                    | ISA        | Example register names     |
                    | ---------- | -------------------------- |
                    | **x86-64** | `rax`, `rbx`, `rcx`, `rdx` |
                    | **ARM64**  | `x0`, `x1`, `x2`, `x3`     |
                    | **RISC-V** | `x0`, `x1`, `x2`, `x3`     |

                Even when names look similar, the rules may be different.


One more detail:

    Architecture can mean different things depending on context.

    Sometimes people say:

        “ARM architecture”
        and they mean the ARM ISA.

    Other times, “CPU architecture” can mean the broader internal design of the CPU, including cores, cache,
    pipelines, registers, and so on. That broader internal design is often called microarchitecture.


x86-64 and ARM both are as ISA families. What make them same and different?

    Yes. x86-64 and ARM are both CPU instruction-set architecture families, meaning both define rules for how machine code talks to a CPU.
    But they are not the same language. They are like two different CPU “languages.”

        A small correction first:

            x86-64 is more specifically the modern 64-bit version of the x86 family.
            ARM is a broader family; its modern 64-bit form is usually called ARM64 or AArch64.

        So a cleaner comparison is often:

            x86-64 vs ARM64 / AArch64

    What makes them the same?

        - Both x86-64 and ARM define a CPU abstract machine.

            They both define things like:

                | Both define                       | Meaning                                                          |
                | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
                | **Instructions**                  | Add, subtract, load, store, jump, compare, etc.                  |
                | **Registers**                     | Small fast CPU storage locations                                 |
                | **Machine-code encoding**         | How instructions are represented as `0` and `1`                  |
                | **Memory access rules**           | How data is loaded from and stored to memory                     |
                | **Control flow**                  | How programs jump, call functions, and return                    |
                | **Privilege/system rules**        | How operating systems control memory, interrupts, and protection |
                | **Atomic operations**             | Instructions for multicore/thread synchronization                |
                | **Floating-point/vector support** | Instructions for math, graphics, media, AI, etc.                 |

        - Both can run modern operating systems.

            For example:

                Windows
                Linux
                macOS
                Android
                iOS

            can run on CPUs based on one of these architectures, depending on the version.

        - Both can run programs written in languages like:

            C, C++, Rust, Go, Java, Python, JavaScript

            but the program must eventually be compiled or translated into the correct machine code for that CPU.

    What make them different?
    
        The biggest difference is:

            x86-64 and ARM use different instructions, different binary encodings, and different CPU rules.

            So machine code made for one does not directly run on the other:

                x86-64 machine code  → x86-64 CPU
                ARM64 machine code   → ARM64 CPU

                If you try this:

                    x86-64 machine code → ARM64 CPU

                    it will not run directly.

                You need:

                    recompilation
                    or
                    emulation
                    or
                    binary translation

                    Example: Apple Silicon Macs use ARM-based CPUs, but they can run many older x86-64 Mac apps using translation.

        Main differences:

            1. Instruction design style: CISC vs RISC

                Traditionally:

                    x86-64 = CISC-style
                    ARM    = RISC-style

                x86-64: CISC-style

                    CISC means Complex Instruction Set Computer. x86-64 has many instructions, and
                    some instructions can do more complex things.

                    Example idea:

                        asm(assembly)
                        
                            add [memory_address], rax

                        This can add a register value directly to a value in memory.
                        So one instruction can combine:

                            read memory
                            add value
                            write result

                ARM: RISC-style

                    RISC means Reduced Instruction Set Computer. ARM usually uses simpler instructions.
                    Arithmetic normally happens between registers, not directly on memory.

                    Example idea:

                        asm(assembly)

                            ldr x1, [memory_address]   ; load from memory
                            add x1, x1, x2             ; add registers
                            str x1, [memory_address]   ; store back to memory

                        So ARM often separates:

                            load
                            calculate
                            store

                Conclution:

                    - x86-64 (CISC-style): one instruction can combine many instructions & one instruction can do more complex things.
                    - ARM (RISC-style): often separates instructions to make simpler instructions.

            2. Registers

                Registers are tiny fast storage locations inside the CPU.
                x86-64 has fewer(lebih sedikit) general-purpose registers than ARM64.

                Example:

                    | Architecture | General-purpose registers                                                |
                    | ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
                    | **x86-64**   | 16 main 64-bit registers, such as `rax`, `rbx`, `rcx`, `rdx`, `r8`–`r15` |
                    | **ARM64**    | 31 main 64-bit registers, `x0`–`x30`, plus special registers             |

                    This means ARM64 code often has more registers available for holding temporary values.

                        Example x86-64 registers:
                            
                            asm(assembly)

                                rax
                                rbx
                                rcx
                                rdx
                                rsi
                                rdi
                                rsp
                                rbp
                                r8
                                r9
                                ...

                        Example ARM64 registers:

                            asm(assembly)

                                x0
                                x1
                                x2
                                x3
                                ...
                                x30
            
            3. Instruction encoding

                x86-64 instructions are variable length.

                    An x86-64 instruction can be different sizes, such as:
                        
                        1 byte
                        2 bytes
                        3 bytes
                        ...
                        up to 15 bytes

                ARM instructions are usually more regular.

                    For example, modern ARM64 commonly uses fixed-size 32-bit instructions.
                    So:
                        | Architecture        | Instruction size style           |
                        | ------------------- | -------------------------------- |
                        | **x86-64**          | Variable-length instructions     |
                        | **ARM64 / AArch64** | Mostly fixed 32-bit instructions |

                Conclution:

                    x86-64 CPUs need more complex instruction decoders.
                    ARM decoding is usually more regular.

            4. Memory access style

                x86-64 allows many instructions to work directly with memory.

                    Example:

                        asm(assembly)
                        
                            add rax, [rbx]

                        One instruction combine many instructions

                            This means:

                                load value from memory address in rbx
                                add it to rax
                                store result in rax
                
                ARM64 is more strictly load/store.

                    Example:

                        asm(assembly)

                            ldr x1, [x2]
                            add x0, x0, x1

                        This means:

                            load memory value into x1
                            then add registers

                        So, they can not work directly with memory at once

                Conclution:

                    | Architecture | Memory style                                                               |
                    | ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
                    | **x86-64**   | Many instructions can use memory directly                                  |
                    | **ARM64**    | Load/store architecture: memory access mostly uses load/store instructions |

