    ISA families

        An ISA family is a group of related CPU instruction rulebooks.

            For example:

                | ISA family        | Examples inside the family   | Common CPUs                                   |
                | ----------------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
                | **x86 family**    | x86, IA-32, x86-64           | Intel Core, AMD Ryzen                         |
                | **ARM family**    | ARMv7, ARMv8, ARMv9, AArch64 | Apple M chips, Snapdragon, Raspberry Pi       |
                | **RISC-V family** | RV32, RV64, plus extensions  | microcontrollers, research CPUs, custom chips |

            So an ISA family is not always one exact ISA. It is more like a family tree of related instruction systems.

        What makes ISA families different?

            They differ in the instruction design style, register, instruction size, instruction decoding, Memory access style.

            1. Different instructions
            Each ISA family has its own instruction names and behavior.

                For example, addition can look different:

                    asm(assembly)

                        ; x86-64
                        add rax, rbx
                        
                        ; ARM / AArch64
                        add x0, x1, x2
                        
                        ; RISC-V
                        add x1, x2, x3

                    They all mean “add,” but the exact rules are different.

            2. Different machine-code encoding
            Even if two CPUs both have an “add” instruction, the binary pattern may be different.

                For example:

                    x86-64 ADD      -> one binary encoding
                    ARM ADD         -> different binary encoding
                    RISC-V ADD      -> different binary encoding

                That is why a program compiled for x86-64 cannot directly run on ARM. The CPU would read the bits differently.

            3. Different registers
            Registers are tiny fast storage locations inside the CPU. Different ISA families expose different registers.

                For example:

                    | ISA        | Example register names     |
                    | ---------- | -------------------------- |
                    | **x86-64** | `rax`, `rbx`, `rcx`, `rdx` |
                    | **ARM64**  | `x0`, `x1`, `x2`, `x3`     |
                    | **RISC-V** | `x0`, `x1`, `x2`, `x3`     |

                Even when names look similar, the rules may be different.

            4. Different instruction style
            A major difference is CISC vs RISC style.

                x86 / x86-64: CISC-like
                x86 has many complex instructions. Some instructions can do multiple things.

                    Example idea:

                        asm(assembly)
                        
                            add [memory_location], rax

                    This can add a register value directly to something in memory.

                ARM and RISC-V: RISC-like
                ARM and RISC-V usually use simpler instructions. They often separate memory access from arithmetic.

                    Example idea:

                        asm(assembly)

                            load value from memory into register
                            add registers
                            store result back to memory

            5. Different compatibility
            Programs must be compiled for the correct ISA.

                For example:

                    Program compiled for x86-64  -> runs on x86-64 CPU
                    Program compiled for ARM64   -> runs on ARM64 CPU
                    Program compiled for RISC-V  -> runs on RISC-V CPU

                If you try to run ARM machine code on an x86-64 CPU, it will not work directly.

                    You need one of these:

                        recompile the program
                        or
                        use an emulator/translator

                    Example:
                    
                        Apple Silicon Macs use ARM-based chips, but they can run many x86-64 Mac apps through translation software.




